{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Then shall the priest take for him who is to be cleansed two birds.</b> Above, in the section of תַּזְרִיעַ, I wrote that the essence of tzara'at comes upon three transgressions hinted at in the portion, and they are: evil speech, arrogance, and craving for wealth. And accordingly we may say that the birds atone for three: for evil speech, because they perform the act of chirping, etc. (Arakhin 16a); and for arrogance, which rises upward like birds flying (Isaiah 31:5); and for the craving of wealth, concerning which it is said (Proverbs 23:4–5): ״אַל תִּיגַע לְהַעֲשִׁיר וְגוֹ׳ כִּי עָשֹׂה יַעֲשֶׂה לּוֹ כְנָפַיִם״ and so forth. And since most of the commentators have elaborated at length in their own way in this portion regarding the sin of evil speech, I too shall add a good lesson, to explain what reason's net will yield from the three proclamations that we find among our Rabbis, of blessed memory, who speak concerning the sin of evil speech." "The first proclamation is what is brought in the Midrash Rabbah (Metzora 16:2): A story of a certain peddler who would go around the villages near Tzippori, and he would proclaim and say: Who wants to buy a life-potion? Come and I will give it to the whole world. The commentator Ba'al Matnot Kehunah explains: they looked after him — from the language of ״וְהִבִּיטוּ אַחֲרֵי מֹשֶׁה״ (Exodus 33:8). Rabbi Yannai was sitting and studying in his triclinium. He said to him: Come up here, sell it to me. He said to him: You have no need for it, nor do those like you. He pressed him, so he went up to him. He brought out for him a scroll of Psalms, wrapped, and showed him this verse: ״מִי הָאִישׁ הֶחָפֵץ חַיִּים וְגוֹ׳ נְצֹר לְשׁוֹנְךָ מֵרָע״ (Psalms 34:13–14). Rabbi Yannai said: Solomon also proclaims: ״שֹׁמֵר פִּיו וּלְשׁוֹנוֹ שֹׁמֵר מִצָּרוֹת נַפְשׁוֹ״ (Proverbs 21:23) — he guards from tzara'at of his soul. Rabbi Yannai said: All my days I would read this verse and I did not know where it is pashut, until this peddler came and informed me. Therefore Moshe warns Israel: ״זֹאת תִּהְיֶה תּוֹרַת הַמְּצֹרָע״ — the one who brings forth ra'. Thus far its language." "The second proclamation we find in tractate Avodah Zarah (19a): Rabbi Alexandri proclaimed: Who wants life, who wants life? They would gather to him, the whole world and come to him. They said to him: Give us life. He said to them: ״מִי הָאִישׁ הֶחָפֵץ חַיִּים, נְצוֹר לְשׁוֹנְךָ מֵרָע״. Lest a person say: Since I have guarded my tongue from evil, I shall go and sleep — the Talmud teaches to say: ״סוּר מֵרָע וַעֲשֵׂה טוֹב״. And 'good' is nothing but Torah, as it is said: ״כִּי לֶקַח טוֹב נָתַתִּי לָכֶם״ and so forth (Proverbs 4:2)." "Now, regarding the peddler's proclamation, one must press: what difference does it make to us whether he was a peddler or a shopkeeper, that it was necessary to inform us that the proclaimer was a peddler? Also the expression מְחַזֵּר requires explanation, for this term applies to one who goes around behind him, and it should have said מְסוֹבֵב. And what difference does it make to us that the villages were near Tzippori? Furthermore, there are many differences between the peddler's proclamation and the proclamation of Rabbi Alexandri: for the peddler proclaimed, 'Who wants to buy a life-potion?' — he mentioned 'to buy,' whereas Rabbi Alexandri proclaimed, 'Who wants life?' — he did not mention buying. Nor did the peddler mention any potion. With the peddler it says, 'Come and I will give it to the whole world' — implying that no one sought to purchase anything from him; rather they looked after him. Whereas with Rabbi Alexandri: he gathers the whole world and they say, 'Give us life.' And what does it matter that it told us Rabbi Yannai was sitting in his triclinium? And what does it matter that it told us he showed him a scroll of Psalms that was wrapped — whether it was wrapped or unwrapped? And what was Rabbi Yannai's intention when he added for him a proof from Solomon, who said: ״שׁוֹמֵר פִּיו וּלְשׁוֹנוֹ״ and so forth? And why did he say that all his days he had not known where this verse is pashut and so forth — surely even the little children in the schoolhouse know how to explain it? And if according to the words of the Ba'al HaAkedah who explained in this portion that the difficulty was the direct address followed by the indirect — for it begins in indirect language: ״מִי הָאִישׁ הֶחָפֵץ חַיִּים״, and afterward speaks to them directly: ״נְצוֹר לְשׁוֹנְךָ מֵרָע״ — but surely the intent of the verse is that the matter should be in the manner of a proclamation: the proclaimer proclaims simply in indirect language, and when the buyers come to buy, then he speaks with them directly. In any case it is difficult: he should have understood all this from the proclamation of Rabbi Alexandri." "And what seems closest to me to say, in resolving all these difficulties, is what we find in tractate Arakhin (15b): Rabbi Chama bar Chanina said: What is the remedy for one who gossips in evil speech? If he is a Torah scholar, let him occupy himself with Torah, as it is said (Proverbs 15:4) ״מַרְפֵּא לָשׁוֹן עֵץ חַיִּים״. And 'tongue' is nothing but the evil tongue, as it is said (Jeremiah 9:7) ״חֵץ שָׁחוּט לְשׁוֹנָם״. And 'tree of life' is nothing but Torah, as it is said (Proverbs 3:18) ״עֵץ חַיִּים הִיא״ and so forth. And if he is an ignoramus, let him humble himself, as it is said (ibid. 15:4) ״וְסֶלֶף בָּהּ שֶׁבֶר בְּרוּחַ״. Rabbi Abba bar Chanina said: One who gossips has no remedy, for David has already cut him down by the holy spirit, as it is said (Psalms 12:4) ״יַכְרֵת ה׳ כָּל שִׂפְתֵי חֲלָקוֹת לָשׁוֹן מְדַבֶּרֶת גְּדוֹלוֹת״. Rather, what is his remedy — so that he not come to evil speech in the first place? If he is a Torah scholar, let him occupy himself with Torah, and so forth." "And it appears that the reason of Rabbi Abba is that justice dictates that there should be no remedy for him, for if he has already spoken evil gossip and inflicted a blemish on the consecrated and brought an evil report, and the outcry has spread throughout the entire world — it is obvious that there is no remedy for the one about whom the evil gossip was spoken. And even if the speaker were to offer all the rams of Nevayoth, he cannot restore the evil thought and words that went forth. And shall he go from one end of the heavens to the other and proclaim: 'I spoke falsely'? — as is the custom of our generation, that if one of the masters of evil gossip brings disgrace upon some honored person, swiftly his word runs from the rising of the sun to its setting, and he himself cannot repair what he has distorted. Therefore it is just: just as there is no remedy for the one about whom he spoke, so too there is no remedy for the speaker. Therefore David cut him off in the holy spirit and said: ״יַכְרֵת ה׳ כָּל שִׂפְתֵי חֲלָקוֹת״ and so forth, and he gave the reason for the matter: ״מִשּׁוֹד עֲנִיִּים מֵאֶנְקַת אֶבְיוֹנִים״ and so forth — these are the stricken ones who cry the cry of the wounded, the poor and destitute upon whom the venom went forth from a ready-tongued arrow, who have no remedy. Therefore there is no remedy for the smooth lips either. And of this he said (Psalms 101:5) ״מְלָשְׁנִי בַסֵּתֶר רֵעֵהוּ אוֹתוֹ אַצְמִית״, and we translate it 'לַחֲלוּטִין' — for he remains thus נִשָּׁאֵר לַחֲלוּטִין, and there is no remedy for him." "But Rabbi Ḥama held: since it says ״מַרְפֵּא״, there is no healing except in a place of illness — therefore he wished to interpret the verse of one who has already spoken. And as an allusion corresponding to this dispute we find (Ketubot 77b): Rabbi Yoḥanan proclaimed: Beware of the flies of those afflicted with ra'atan, for it is a species of leprosy. And they conclude there: Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi joined with them and was engaged in Torah, etc. And the question arises: what did Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi see to rely on a miracle for nothing? And it appears to me by way of allusion, that they dispute in accordance with the dispute of Rabbi Abba and Rabbi Ḥama, for 'flies' is a euphemism for the masters of evil gossip, who act like the fly, whose way is to pass over every clean place on the body, and when it finds some place that is soiled or a fresh wound, there it alights upon the site of the sore. So too the masters of evil gossip: if they see some man full of wisdom and honor, they never speak of his virtues; rather, they seek out the blemishes of human beings — for there is no man righteous on earth who does not stumble in some minor folly." "And of them Solomon said: ״זְבוּבֵי מָוֶת יַבְאִישׁ״ and so forth (Ecclesiastes 10:1) — that is, these masters of evil gossip who were likened to flies, ״וּבְרוּחַ שְׂפָתָיו יָמִית״, for evil gossip kills three (Leviticus Rabbah 5:10); therefore he called them 'flies of death.' And he said ״יַבְאִישׁ״, for their entire intent is to putrefy the reputation of good people. ״יַבִּיעַ שֶׁמֶן רוֹקֵחַ״ — ״יַבִּיעַ״ is an expression of speech, and he means to say that even from a perfect man whose fragrance diffuses like perfumed oil, nevertheless he endeavors to putrefy his reputation. ״יָקָר מֵחָכְמָה מִכָּבוֹד סִכְלוּת מְעָט״ — for in his eyes, that minor folly he sees in his fellow is precious and honorable in his eyes more than all the wisdom and honor he sees in him, to the point that this minor folly outweighs all the wisdom and the honor. And he gave the reason for the matter, for ״לֵב חָכָם לִימִינוֹ״ (Ecclesiastes 10:2) — that is, to judge created beings favorably, as they said, blessed be they (Tanchuma Shemot 18): these incline toward merit, etc.; ״וְלֵב כְּסִיל לִשְׂמֹאלוֹ״ — to judge created beings toward liability — therefore he never speaks of his fellow's virtues but only of his disgrace. And the reason for this: ״גַם בְּדֶרֶךְ שֶׁהַסָּכָל הוֹלֵךְ לִבּוֹ חָסֵר וְאָמַר לַכֹּל סָכָל הוּא״ (ibid. 10:3) — he means to say: whoever disqualifies does so with his own blemish (Kiddushin 70b), and this fool who suspects the worthy knows within himself that that deficiency and reproach he casts upon his fellow exists also within him, and he too is lacking in his heart regarding that matter. Therefore he says of everyone that he is a fool, and he attributes to others too that folly which he knows resides first and foremost in himself — for he supposes that since he acts thus, everyone acts likewise. These are the flies that constantly investigate the blemishes and deficiencies of created beings, in the manner spoken of (Exodus 33:8) ״וְהִבִּיטוּ אַחֲרֵי מֹשֶׁה״; therefore they were stricken with leprosy called ra'atan, for just as they are masters of ra'atan who look and scrutinize after the blemishes of human beings, so they were stricken with leprosy called ra'atan. And they said: beware of them — not to engage with them, to turn them from their evil way or to instruct them in the path of repentance, for he who holds 'spoke' holds there is no remedy; therefore one must not engage with them for nothing. And so too is the opinion of Rabbi Zeira and Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Asi, who distanced themselves from their territory with every manner of distancing, as is concluded there." "And it could be that the reason for this extreme distancing was so that they would not suffer harm from the ready-tongued arrow, as is said (Psalms 101:7): ״לֹא יֵשֵׁב בְּקֶרֶב בֵּיתִי עֹשֵׂה רְמִיָּה״ — specifically in his actual house he shall not dwell; but ״דֹּבֵר שְׁקָרִים לֹא יִכּוֹן לְנֶגֶד עֵינָי״ — even from afar, as far as the range of a bowshot of their ready-tongued arrow, like the aim of a tongue opposite, that is, because the tongue harms even from afar. But Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi held that there is a remedy even if he has already spoken, if he is a Torah scholar let him engage in Torah, etc. — and this is what is concluded: Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi joined with them and engaged in Torah, etc., for he held that through Torah study there is a remedy even for one who has already spoken, to the point that he has already been stricken with leprosy." "Now Rabbi Alexandri also held 'spoke — there is no remedy'; therefore he went only to the gatherings of those who had not yet spoken, and he would warn them and teach them the way of preserving health, so that they would not come to evil gossip, and he would proclaim: 'מַאן בָּעֵי חַיֵּי, מַאן בָּעֵי חַיֵּי' — he did not use the word 'remedy,' nor did he use the word 'to buy,' for preserving health requires no remedy; rather, the physician merely teaches him how to be helped — that he guard himself from harmful things so as not to come to illness. Therefore it is not fitting that the teacher should seek payment for his words, for just as he received it for free — for speech was given to him as a gift — so too he should teach the ways of healing to his fellow for free. But one who has already fallen into illness needs medicines, and on their account the physician demands his fee, for they too did not come to him for free, but he paid their price or exerted himself for them. And since Rabbi Alexandri went to the gatherings of the worthy who had not spoken evil gossip, to teach them the way of preserving health so that they would not come to the sin of the tongue, therefore he proclaimed simply 'מַאן בָּעֵי חַיֵּי, מַאן בָּעֵי חַיֵּי.' And he doubled the matter to tell you that there is a remedy for this one — that he not come to speak evil gossip — and for that one — that evil gossip not be spoken about him. All the world gathered and said to him: 'הַב לָן חַיֵּי,' for they too desired the thing in which they had already been practiced, and they sought to know the path that leads to preserving health. He said to them the verse: ״מִי הָאִישׁ הֶחָפֵץ חַיִּים״ and so forth, ״נְצֹר לְשׁוֹנְךָ מֵרָע״ and so forth. And since preserving health requires no remedy at all, therefore it is concluded: lest you say I shall go and hire myself in sleep to guard my tongue by sitting idle and doing nothing at all — a hermeneutic teaches ״וַעֲשֵׂה טוֹב״, and 'good' is nothing but Torah, etc. (Avot 6:3) — that is to say, even though he does not need medicines and great repentance, he still needs to guard his tongue through Torah study if he is a scholar; but concerning the ignorant he said afterward ״קָרוֹב ה׳ לְנִשְׁבְּרֵי לֵב״, as is written: ״וְסֶלֶף בָּהּ שֶׁבֶר בְּרוּחַ״ (Proverbs 15:4)." "But this peddler held that there is a remedy even for one who has already spoken, and he knew this from his own experience, for he too had been a peddler going about with talebearing, accustomed with his tongue to stir up strife between brothers. And afterward he set his heart to return in repentance, and he sought out the paths of healing that our Rabbis of blessed memory mentioned: for a Torah scholar, through the study of Torah; for an ignoramus, through a broken spirit. And he saw that these remedies helped him. Therefore his heart was filled to bring merit to the many and to restore in repentance all those towns where he knew there were slanderers — before the days of evil come upon them with leprosy and they need to go about seeking birds that fly, like the purification of the metzora with birds that perform the act of chirping. And this is what it says, that he would go around the towns adjacent to Tzippori: he would go around calling to repentance those towns close to the matter of the bird that performs the act of chirping, and he would proclaim: 'Who wishes to buy a life-potion?' — for they were like sick people needing medicines for a cure, which require purchase; therefore he said 'to buy a life-potion.' And for this reason he does not raise in this proclamation 'perhaps you will say: I will go and sleep,' etc. — for from where would healing come through sitting idle? And do not make one who is already sick. But in the proclamation of Rabbi Alexandroi it fits well as stated, for there the novelty is that the study of Torah is required, whereas here the novelty is that the study of Torah helps even for one who has already spoken. And the peddler did not repeat his proclamation to say twice 'Who wishes to buy a life-potion,' as Rabbi Alexandroi repeated his words, because even he concedes that the evil report he spoke has no remedy at all, and he sought a remedy only for this one who has already spoken — unlike Rabbi Alexandroi, who sought a remedy for both. They turned their backs on him — for they had already chosen for themselves this corrupt path and were accustomed on their tongues to speak evil speech; therefore they did not wish to buy anything from him at all. And moreover, they turned their backs and looked behind him, for they were faultfinders who look after the many blemishes of souls and their deficiencies. For so is the way of those who despise reproof: they look behind the one who rebukes them to find in him some slight disqualification, in order to answer him 'remove the beam,' etc. Therefore they said to him: Yesterday you were a peddler going about with talebearing, and today you wish to correct our ways? Straighten yourself first!" "Rabbi Yannai was sitting and stretching out his legs in his triclinium, like Rabbi Zeira and Rabbi Ami and Rav Ashi, who distanced themselves from the region of the slanderers so as not to be in their company. He said to him: 'Come up here.' He said to him: 'You have no need,' etc. — for he thinks that the verse 'a healing tongue is a tree of life' speaks specifically of one who has already spoken, but you and all like you have no need for guarding. Nevertheless he pressed him to know the remedy: he showed him a scroll of Psalms lying wrapped up, to tell him that because this scroll is wrapped up and set in a corner — no one expounds it, no one seeks it, and no one reads it faithfully — therefore through the path of rebuke they did not know what is said in the verse 'Who is the man who desires life.' Rabbi Yannai said: 'Solomon also proclaims,' etc. — behold the book of Proverbs, which is not wrapped up and set aside, for it is the way of the world to teach the book to youths, as it is written: 'לְנַעַר דַּעַת וּמְזִמָּה' (Proverbs 1:4), and even so they do not do what they read in it — 'שׁוֹמֵר פִּיו וּלְשׁוֹנוֹ' etc. — but rather they certainly know their Master and intend to rebel against Him. And one may say that the Tanna supports him, for it is difficult for him: what did Solomon add to the words of his father? For if the verse 'Who is the man who desires life' speaks of one who has already spoken — that the remedy helps him — you would think that one who has not spoken need not guard his tongue, meaning, through the study of Torah, as Rabbi Alexandroi concludes: 'perhaps you will say: I will go and sleep,' etc. — he teaches us 'שׁוֹמֵר פִּיו וּלְשׁוֹנוֹ,' that even one who has not spoken needs guarding through the study of Torah. But if one who has already spoken has no remedy, then perforce you must say that the verse 'Who is the man who desires life' etc. speaks of one who has not spoken; if so, what did Solomon add in the verse 'שׁוֹמֵר פִּיו וּלְשׁוֹנוֹ' beyond his father's words? Rather, surely it is as you say: that this verse speaks even of one who has already spoken." "And of this Rabbi Yannai said: 'All my days I did not know where this verse is stated,' etc. — and that is the verse 'שׁוֹמֵר פִּיו וּלְשׁוֹנוֹ,' for he had thought that one who has already spoken has no remedy, and if so it was difficult for him what Solomon added to his father's words — until this peddler came and informed him that the verse 'Who is the man who desires life' speaks of one who has already spoken, and if so 'שׁוֹמֵר פִּיו וּלְשׁוֹנוֹ' speaks of one who has not yet spoken. And if you wish to say: in any case Rabbi Yannai is speaking about the peddler's verse — it appears to me that what was difficult for him was the verse in which the response of the class of buyers is missing. For the seller proclaims 'Who is the man who desires life,' and afterward the buyers come and say 'Give us life' or 'Sell us a life-potion,' and afterward he answers: guard your tongue from evil — and why did he omit in these verses the words of the buyers? Rather, surely it is true, as the peddler said, that this verse speaks of classes who have already spoken evil speech and find it hard to abandon the habit; and sometimes they will not wish to purchase the remedy, as indeed happened to the peddler, that they did not wish to buy anything from him. Nevertheless the proclaimer is obligated to make known to them this path of rebuke even if they do not seek it from him. Therefore the verse omitted the words of the buyers, to tell you that even if it were a generation of perverts who would not wish to buy anything at all from the physician and would not seek him out — nevertheless he will seek them out to instruct them in the way of peace, as it is written (Psalms 34:15): 'Seek peace and pursue it.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Vayikra Rabbah 9:9): All the commandments — if one comes your way you are obligated to perform it, but peace is not so; rather, you are obligated to pursue it, as it says 'Seek peace and pursue it.' And this is through 'Turn away from evil and do good' — and there is no 'good' but Torah, etc., for therefore it says that all her paths are peace (Proverbs 3:17), because it shields from evil speech, which causes all manner of strife. And this is what it says: 'Who is the man who desires life, who loves days to see good' — for those faultfinders love the days in which they see the evil they find in their fellows, so that they have material to dwell upon and report from it; but the one who desires life shuts his eyes from seeing evil, and loves the days in which he sees good in his fellow, saying 'Would that all the people of HaShem were prophets' — and this is when he guards his tongue from evil through the study of Torah, which is alluded to in 'and do good,' and there is no 'good' but Torah, etc. And for the ignoramus, through a broken spirit, as it is written (Psalms 34:19): 'HaShem is near to the brokenhearted.'" "And I have gone beyond my bounds to elaborate somewhat on a matter that is not from the plain subject of the perasha, because I have seen that this generation is very, very breaches in the sin of the tongue, and it is an ancient leprosy — for even in the exile of Egypt they were [worthy of punishment] because there were informers among them (Tanhuma Shemot 10), yet they were redeemed in the merit of four things, one of them being that there was no evil speech among them, etc. (Yalkut Shimoni, Emor, remez 613), and in the Second Temple they reverted to their former ways. And still today, in this lengthy exile, the sin of the tongue grows and increases until the wrath of HaShem rises without healing. Therefore I saw fit to speak somewhat in this awakening, to say: perhaps the blind in the camp of the Hebrews will hear and take to heart to repair this breach. And this I expounded in the holy congregation of Lublin on Parashat Zachor in the year [5]362 [1602 CE] in a small gathering of the heads of the people and the sages of all the communities, and I have added to them things of this nature and the like." "<b>Cedar wood and scarlet yarn and hyssop.</b> It should have placed cedar wood next to hyssop, since both are of the species of growing things. What reason was there to insert scarlet yarn between them? And in Parashat Chukat (Numbers 19:6) it is written: 'cedar wood and hyssop and scarlet yarn' — why does it change here? And it appears that since this perasha speaks of repairing evil speech — that the teller, even if he be a lowly person like this worm, nevertheless strikes at a great person who is likened to a cedar, as derived in the Yalkut (Isaiah, remez 500) on the verse: 'Fear not, worm of Jacob' (Isaiah 41:14) — therefore his atonement is 'cedar wood and scarlet yarn and hyssop,' to atone for what the act of the worm did to the cedar, and his remedy is that he humble himself like the hyssop, and then he will not wish to cast a blemish in holy things." "And the reason for two birds is this: a person has two kinds of speech — one forbidden and one commanded. The forbidden one is evil speech and its likeness; this is the bird that is slaughtered over an earthen vessel over living water. For if he is an ignoramus, he will have a broken spirit, as it is stated (Proverbs 15:4): ״וְסֶלֶף בָּהּ שֶׁבֶר בְּרוּחַ״ — for he has the status of an earthen vessel, which has no remedy except through breaking, and corresponding to this it is said that the bird that is slaughtered over an earthen vessel shall be slaughtered. And if he is a scholar, let him engage in Torah, which is compared to living waters; therefore it says: ״עַל מַיִם חַיִּים״. And for this reason ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת״ is said five times in the matter of the metzora, to tell you that one who engages in the five books of the Torah is saved from the tzara'at that comes upon evil speech. ״וְאֵת הַצִּפּוֹר הַחַיָּה״ — this corresponds to chirpings of words in Torah and prayer; but one must join to this speech ״עֵץ אֶרֶז וּשְׁנִי תוֹלַעַת וְאֵזוֹב״, which teaches humility and lowliness, so that even in speech of Torah he will have a broken and crushed heart. And our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Sotah 5a): of an adam it is not said ״וְנִרְפָּא״, but of basar it is said (Leviticus 13:18): ״וּבָשָׂר כִּי יִהְיֶה בְעוֹרוֹ שְׁחִין וְנִרְפָּא״ — for one who has a heart of flesh, soft, has a remedy. And likewise our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Sotah 5a): basar — bushah seruchah rimah, for remembrance of all these things brings one to brokenness of heart and humility; therefore it says: ״וְטָבַל אוֹתָם בְּדַם הַצִּפּוֹר הַשְּׁחוּטָה״, to sprinkle upon him from the two birds, in order to bring upon him two things at once: repentance for the past and correction for the future."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {9 "In comment 4, the phrase 'אַךְ כְּנֶגֶד הָעַם הָאָרֶץ' appears to reflect the earlier comment 3's attribution of 'spoke — no remedy' to Rabbi Alexandri, contrasted with Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi's position. The text transitions from preventive care for the unblemished (who need only Torah, not medicine) to the already-wounded common folk, for whom the remedy is the broken heart that draws HaShem near, per the prooftext from Proverbs.", 12 "The Aramaic phrase אוֹדֵיק עֲלֵיהּ עָלְמָא in comment [0] and אוֹדִיק עֲלֵיהּ in its recurrence are rendered 'they turned their backs on him' following the parallel idiom הביטו אחריו which the author immediately glosses; the expression is Talmudic Aramaic for 'they showed him their backs / turned away.'", 13 "The year שס״״ב is normalized to 5362 AM = 1602 CE; the Hebrew small-year notation שס״ב could theoretically read as 362 or 62, but the context of the Kli Yakar's career in Lublin confirms the fuller reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:18.345338832Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}